From baae25274691fbd79c2059048050b000975cc4c9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: patrickjquinn <patrick.jm.quinn@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 22:36:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 121/122] power: supply: bq256xx: Read the charger state before
 taking interrupts

The interrupt handler notifies only when the state differs from the one
it last saw, and nothing set that state before the first interrupt: it
started zeroed. A charger that is already online at probe raises no
interrupt, so unplugging it reads a state equal to the zeroed one (no
input, not charging, no fault) and nothing is notified. The charger then
stays online for its users until the next plug event. On the Zinwa Q25
this kept the USB controller, which follows the charger's online state,
and its PLL running after the cable came out.

Read the state at probe, before the interrupt is requested.

Signed-off-by: patrickjquinn <patrick.jm.quinn@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/power/supply/bq256xx_charger.c | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/bq256xx_charger.c b/drivers/power/supply/bq256xx_charger.c
index e62ab5a73..98540b106 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/bq256xx_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/bq256xx_charger.c
@@ -1766,6 +1766,10 @@ static int bq256xx_probe(struct i2c_client *client)
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	ret = bq256xx_get_state(bq, &bq->state);
+	if (ret)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "Failed to read the charger state\n");
+
 	if (client->irq) {
 		ret = devm_request_threaded_irq(dev, client->irq, NULL,
 						bq256xx_irq_handler_thread,
